ll node itself at this point  we could install angular or react and  start doing front-end development from  this virtual environment when developing  in the browser you might notice a small  amount of lag when saving files because  that has to happen remotely but when  you're actually writing code it should  feel just like you're working in the  desktop version of es code now coder  gives us a couple gigabytes of memory  and storage space but there's a good  chance you want to go beyond these  limits by hosting your own instance in  the cloud which you can do on AWS GCP  Azure or wherever you want we'll set up  bars on Google cloud and I want to show  you how to set it up for both front-end  development and also for machine  learning specifically with ten surface  start by going to the GCP console and  find that compute engine tab we'll  create our own virtual machine with some  customized settings keep in mind this  will accrue cost on GCP so make sure to  shut it down when you're done using it  and everything is billed to the second  so you only have to have your virtual  machine running when you're actually  using BS code in the cloud the smallest  possible instance is about $4.00 per  month but that's probably not going to  be quite enough to run vs code  comfortably it's recommended to start  with a standard instance with two CPU  cores and 7.5 gigabytes of memory that  will cost you about 6 cents per hour to  run or about $49 per month if you just  leave it running continuously I'm just  going to go with the recommended two CPU  cores here and then we're going to set  the operating system to run 2:16 point  o4 we're going to be accessing it on the  web so we'll make sure to allow HTTP and  HTTPS traffic go ahead and click create  and then you should see a green  checkmark when it's ready the next thing  we want to do is install code server  which is the open source project that  powers coder in order to do that we need  to get access to the command line of  t